WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.568 --> 00:00:03.269
-Our next guest is a talented
actress you know from shows

00:00:03.269 --> 00:00:05.171
such as "Yellowjackets,"
"Castle Rock,"

00:00:05.171 --> 00:00:06.372
and "Mrs. America."

00:00:06.372 --> 00:00:08.707
She stars in Hulu's
limited series "Candy,"

00:00:08.707 --> 00:00:10.577
which premieres on Monday,
May 9th.

00:00:10.577 --> 00:00:11.711
Let's take a look.

00:00:11.711 --> 00:00:14.781
-Are you having an affair
with Alan?

00:00:17.950 --> 00:00:21.053
-No.

00:00:21.053 --> 00:00:25.125
-But you did...didn't you?

00:00:27.827 --> 00:00:30.130
-Yes, but it was
a long time ago.

00:00:37.102 --> 00:00:38.805
-Just a minute.

00:00:38.805 --> 00:00:41.374
-Please welcome to the show
Melanie Lynskey, everyone.

00:00:41.374 --> 00:00:43.376
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:43.376 --> 00:00:53.386 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:53.386 --> 00:00:56.556 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:56.556 --> 00:00:58.357
Melanie,
I'm so happy you're here.

00:00:58.357 --> 00:01:01.160
-Oh, my goodness!
I'm so happy to be here.

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:04.363
I'm a little bit nervous.
This is my first-ever talk show.

00:01:04.363 --> 00:01:06.332
-Well, you're off
to an amazing start.

00:01:06.332 --> 00:01:08.534
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Thank you.

00:01:08.534 --> 00:01:09.769
So supportive.

00:01:09.769 --> 00:01:13.039
-Usually first-timers
fall on the stairs.

00:01:13.039 --> 00:01:14.273
It's -- you've just --
-I was concerned.

00:01:14.273 --> 00:01:16.442
-Everything has been really
gracefully so far.

00:01:16.442 --> 00:01:18.044
-I was like,
"Why am I not in sneakers?"

00:01:18.044 --> 00:01:20.013
[ Laughter ]

00:01:20.013 --> 00:01:22.548
-I've been a huge fan of yours
for a long time,

00:01:22.548 --> 00:01:25.084
but I really love
"Yellowjackets,"

00:01:25.084 --> 00:01:26.686
which was a show
that came out this year.

00:01:26.686 --> 00:01:29.155
-Thank you.
-And for those who haven't

00:01:29.155 --> 00:01:31.591
seen it, I guess
it's sort of hard to explain.

00:01:31.591 --> 00:01:35.962
It's sort of a coming-of-age,
horror, cannibal, plane crash?

00:01:35.962 --> 00:01:37.463
-I think that's perfect.
-Thank you.

00:01:37.463 --> 00:01:40.633
-Yeah, I don't think you need
any more information than that.

00:01:40.633 --> 00:01:43.236
Yeah, it's a very intense show,

00:01:43.236 --> 00:01:46.105
and the response
has been amazing.

00:01:46.105 --> 00:01:49.108
I'm used to doing something
and then having, like,

00:01:49.108 --> 00:01:51.010
a couple of people be like,
"I saw that," you know?

00:01:51.010 --> 00:01:53.980
And then it just kind of,
like, floats away.

00:01:53.980 --> 00:01:55.247
[ Laughter ]

00:01:55.247 --> 00:01:57.750
But to have people watching it
week after week

00:01:57.750 --> 00:01:59.385
and be excited,
it's been amazing.

00:01:59.385 --> 00:02:02.755
-It was one of those old-school,
watching it every week,

00:02:02.755 --> 00:02:05.958
not knowing what was happening,
finding a sort of

00:02:05.958 --> 00:02:08.194
klatch of people who are
watching it along with you.

00:02:08.194 --> 00:02:10.062
-Yeah.
-And it was also really --

00:02:10.062 --> 00:02:13.099
I mean, this is strange to say,
we were saying backstage,

00:02:13.099 --> 00:02:16.202
it's really fun.
I mean, I think that not enough

00:02:16.202 --> 00:02:17.937
cannibalistic stuff
is fun these days.

00:02:17.937 --> 00:02:19.738
-It's true.
It's true.

00:02:19.738 --> 00:02:22.842
There's such judgment on it.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:22.842 --> 00:02:24.344
No, it's really fun.

00:02:24.344 --> 00:02:27.846
I'm happy that I don't have to
play a current-day cannibal.

00:02:27.846 --> 00:02:29.348
-Yeah.
-The person I'm playing

00:02:29.348 --> 00:02:31.817
was once a cannibal, so I don't
have to do any of that stuff.

00:02:31.817 --> 00:02:34.320
-It's a a very nice show
for cannibals who are worried

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:36.422
they might never be able
to set their life straight.

00:02:36.422 --> 00:02:38.557
-Yeah, well...
[ Laughter ]

00:02:38.557 --> 00:02:40.026
I don't know how much
any of them have

00:02:40.026 --> 00:02:43.062
set their life straight,
but, yeah, continue on.

00:02:43.062 --> 00:02:45.497
-Yes, it is --
I should be noted it's --

00:02:45.497 --> 00:02:46.866
You're playing your --

00:02:46.866 --> 00:02:48.567
There's a flashback
to the cannibal,

00:02:48.567 --> 00:02:50.269
so you are -- you're past it.

00:02:50.269 --> 00:02:51.670
-Yeah, and there's another
actress who plays

00:02:51.670 --> 00:02:54.440
the younger version of me,
and she's absolutely amazing.

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:56.376
-It's exceptionally cast.
-Yeah.

00:02:56.376 --> 00:02:59.012
-The young people
are exceptionally cast

00:02:59.012 --> 00:03:00.613
for their current-day
counterparts.

00:03:00.613 --> 00:03:02.415
-Yeah, it's absolutely crazy.

00:03:02.415 --> 00:03:04.250
They did a great job.
-It is very cliffhanger-y.

00:03:04.250 --> 00:03:07.353
And I've heard that your
husband, Jason Ritter --

00:03:07.353 --> 00:03:10.156
a very talented actor --
and your friends did try to,

00:03:10.156 --> 00:03:12.124
like, press you for details.

00:03:12.124 --> 00:03:14.193
-It got to a point where
especially there's

00:03:14.193 --> 00:03:17.129
this one mystery
about a character called Adam,

00:03:17.129 --> 00:03:19.599
and everybody was wondering,
"Who's Adam? Who's Adam?"

00:03:19.599 --> 00:03:21.667
And I would pick up my phone,
and my group texts

00:03:21.667 --> 00:03:24.103
with my best girlfriends,
they're all, like,

00:03:24.103 --> 00:03:26.673
discussing it as though
I'm not even on this text.

00:03:26.673 --> 00:03:28.140
[ Laughter ]

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:30.309
And then every now and again,
they'll remember and be like,

00:03:30.309 --> 00:03:32.445
"Melanie, you know,
send an emoji

00:03:32.445 --> 00:03:34.246
if we're getting close,"
or whatever.

00:03:34.246 --> 00:03:37.550
And I'm just like,
"Please, I need one area."

00:03:37.550 --> 00:03:39.285
And, you know, Jason's like,
"Tell me. Don't tell me!

00:03:39.285 --> 00:03:41.220
Do tell me.
Don't tell me!"

00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:42.254
[ Sighs ]

00:03:42.254 --> 00:03:44.523
-Do you like horror as a genre?

00:03:44.523 --> 00:03:46.392
Do you enjoy it?
-I do.

00:03:46.392 --> 00:03:49.762
I love watching horror movies,

00:03:49.762 --> 00:03:52.898
but I don't really get,
like, jump scared.

00:03:52.898 --> 00:03:54.767
-Why would you say
you don't jump scare?

00:03:54.767 --> 00:03:55.868
-I don't know.
There's something, like,

00:03:55.868 --> 00:03:58.771
with my adrenal function,
I guess.

00:03:58.771 --> 00:04:00.807
Something's wrong.
-So you're impossible --

00:04:00.807 --> 00:04:04.344
you're impossible to scare?
-It's -- Well, now I'm nervous

00:04:04.344 --> 00:04:06.145
that someone's gonna --
-No, no, no!

00:04:06.145 --> 00:04:07.980
-...sneak up behind me.
-That's not how we do it.

00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:10.683
-Because of Ellen DeGeneres.
-Yeah.

00:04:10.683 --> 00:04:11.884
Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:11.884 --> 00:04:14.320
-All right.
No, it doesn't really happen.

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:17.724
And it's the worst thing
for my husband, because he --

00:04:17.724 --> 00:04:20.326
his whole life, his favorite
thing has been scaring people.

00:04:20.326 --> 00:04:22.128
He'll hide around corners.
-Yeah.

00:04:22.128 --> 00:04:24.630
-His siblings were tortured
through their whole childhood,

00:04:24.630 --> 00:04:27.466
and they're still a bit shaky.

00:04:27.466 --> 00:04:29.235
And he tries to scare me.

00:04:29.235 --> 00:04:31.304
One time,
he said he was going to bed,

00:04:31.304 --> 00:04:32.772
and I said,
"All right, good night,"

00:04:32.772 --> 00:04:36.008
and I stayed up for 20 minutes.
And when I walked into the room,

00:04:36.008 --> 00:04:38.844
the room was dark,
and he came out on his knees

00:04:38.844 --> 00:04:42.181
like this, like a scary
Leprechaun or something --

00:04:42.181 --> 00:04:44.884
I don't know what he was
trying to do, but I said,

00:04:44.884 --> 00:04:46.252
"Oh, that's scary."

00:04:46.252 --> 00:04:47.753
[ Laughter ]

00:04:47.753 --> 00:04:49.522
'Cause it looked weird.
-Yeah.

00:04:49.522 --> 00:04:51.924
Although, I will say, if he's
been doing it his whole life,

00:04:51.924 --> 00:04:53.893
I feel like he should be
a little better.

00:04:53.893 --> 00:04:55.761
[ Laughter ]

00:04:55.761 --> 00:04:58.263
-You're not wrong.
He could have, like --

00:04:58.263 --> 00:04:59.732
he could have had some --

00:04:59.732 --> 00:05:01.500
something a little bit scarier
than that.

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:03.235
-Yeah, like, I mean,
a Leprechaun costume, I think,

00:05:03.235 --> 00:05:04.537
would have put that
through the roof.

00:05:04.537 --> 00:05:07.139
-He should have just shelled out
for a costume.

00:05:07.139 --> 00:05:09.108
Yeah.
-Maybe it's just a dry run.

00:05:09.108 --> 00:05:10.909
Maybe to prepare.
-Yeah.

00:05:10.909 --> 00:05:13.245
-Next St. Patrick's day, though,
you know...

00:05:13.245 --> 00:05:15.515
Be on your toes.
-Exactly.

00:05:15.515 --> 00:05:18.484
-So this --
Your new show, "Candy,"

00:05:18.484 --> 00:05:20.352
this is based
on a real-life story.

00:05:20.352 --> 00:05:23.222
So I feel like it's not --
it doesn't count as a spoiler.

00:05:23.222 --> 00:05:25.224
-Yeah.
-Because you and Jessica Biel

00:05:25.224 --> 00:05:27.826
play a couple of friends,
one of whom

00:05:27.826 --> 00:05:29.295
murders the other one
with an ax.

00:05:29.295 --> 00:05:30.495
-Yes.
-Okay.

00:05:30.495 --> 00:05:31.597
-Yes.
-I hope I haven't given

00:05:31.597 --> 00:05:33.833
too much away.
-No, I think, I mean,

00:05:33.833 --> 00:05:36.703
a lot of people know the story,
and from the first episode,

00:05:36.703 --> 00:05:39.138
it's pretty clear
that that's what happens.

00:05:39.138 --> 00:05:40.505
I'm the one who gets murdered.

00:05:40.505 --> 00:05:41.674
-Okay.
Gotcha.

00:05:41.674 --> 00:05:43.609
I wasn't gonna give it away,
but congrats!

00:05:43.609 --> 00:05:46.111
-Well, thanks.
Thank you.

00:05:46.111 --> 00:05:49.382
-Also, congratulations on the --
I guess this --

00:05:49.382 --> 00:05:51.150
is this early '80s
that it takes place?

00:05:51.150 --> 00:05:53.085
-1980.
-Okay. Gotcha.

00:05:53.085 --> 00:05:56.022
And, really, just the wigs
are incredible.

00:05:56.022 --> 00:05:58.023
-Mm-hmm.
-That's a fantastic --

00:05:58.023 --> 00:06:00.460
And one really,
based on how it frames her face,

00:06:00.460 --> 00:06:01.560
you wonder why Jessica Biel

00:06:01.560 --> 00:06:02.428
hasn't been doing this
full time.

00:06:02.428 --> 00:06:03.662
[ Laughter ]
-I know.

00:06:03.662 --> 00:06:06.798
She somehow
still looks beautiful.

00:06:06.798 --> 00:06:07.933
I don't really know...

00:06:07.933 --> 00:06:09.368
-Yeah, she really does
still pull it off.

00:06:09.368 --> 00:06:12.571
-I'm not pulling it off quite
like she is, but she's really...

00:06:12.571 --> 00:06:14.039
I did press with her
a while ago,

00:06:14.039 --> 00:06:15.207
and so many people were like,

00:06:15.207 --> 00:06:17.443
"You look like your husband
in 2001."

00:06:17.443 --> 00:06:18.910
I was like...
[ Laughter ]

00:06:18.910 --> 00:06:20.880
I never saw it before.

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:23.516
-I didn't either, but, oh, my
God, that is definitely it.

00:06:23.516 --> 00:06:25.751
-Yeah, that's it.
Yeah.

00:06:25.751 --> 00:06:28.587
-And you have a daughter now?
-I do, yes.

00:06:28.587 --> 00:06:29.888
-3 years old.
-3-year-old, yeah.

00:06:29.888 --> 00:06:31.924
-So that means, like,
a sort of --

00:06:31.924 --> 00:06:34.327
I guess, 1-year-old
when the pandemic started?

00:06:34.327 --> 00:06:35.561
-Yeah.
-Okay. Gotcha.

00:06:35.561 --> 00:06:37.129
-She's very good
with her little mask.

00:06:37.129 --> 00:06:38.731
-Does your husband
ever try to scare her?

00:06:38.731 --> 00:06:41.834
-He tries to scare her
all the time.

00:06:41.834 --> 00:06:46.205
She is much more like in line
with his own sort of fear level,

00:06:46.205 --> 00:06:47.739
which is like,
I can be in the kitchen

00:06:47.739 --> 00:06:49.341
drinking a cup of coffee,

00:06:49.341 --> 00:06:51.543
and he'll walk in
and be like, "Aah!"

00:06:51.543 --> 00:06:53.880
So she's more like that.
-Okay. Gotcha.

00:06:53.880 --> 00:06:57.049
-She's easily scared,
but she hates it.

00:06:57.049 --> 00:06:59.151
She hates being scared.
And so I'll say, you know,

00:06:59.151 --> 00:07:01.787
"A good thing to say is
'Dada, get ahold of yourself.'"

00:07:01.787 --> 00:07:03.422
[ Laughter ]
And she remembers.

00:07:03.422 --> 00:07:05.524
She's really good.
So next time she sees him,

00:07:05.524 --> 00:07:07.826
she'll be like,
"Dada, get ahold of yourself."

00:07:07.826 --> 00:07:10.162
And he'll be like,
"Where'd that come from?"

00:07:10.162 --> 00:07:11.597
[ Laughter ]

00:07:11.597 --> 00:07:13.533
-Speaking of babies, you have --
this is not even

00:07:13.533 --> 00:07:17.269
a line of yours, but you were in
the film "Sweet Home Alabama,"

00:07:17.269 --> 00:07:21.874
and this is, like, just sort of
an iconic line in the film,

00:07:21.874 --> 00:07:24.877
where Reese Witherspoon said...
-"Look at you."

00:07:24.877 --> 00:07:26.646
-Yeah.
"You have a baby in a bar."

00:07:26.646 --> 00:07:29.081
-Mm-hmm.
-And I feel like this

00:07:29.081 --> 00:07:30.249
might have even been
in the trailer.

00:07:30.249 --> 00:07:31.617
I feel like...
-I think so.

00:07:31.617 --> 00:07:34.420
-Yes. And do people still
recognize you from this moment?

00:07:34.420 --> 00:07:36.456
-People recognize me
from this moment.

00:07:36.456 --> 00:07:38.858
People quote this line
that's not --

00:07:38.858 --> 00:07:41.227
And I say, "Oh, Reese did a
great job with that line."

00:07:41.227 --> 00:07:43.362
It's so weird.
You know.

00:07:43.362 --> 00:07:46.598
But I think Reese Witherspoon,
she can make anything iconic.

00:07:46.598 --> 00:07:48.233
-Yeah.
-She's got that talent.

00:07:48.233 --> 00:07:50.803
-Is it nice that you --
I guess because of the pandemic,

00:07:50.803 --> 00:07:53.272
you never had to risk bringing
your own baby to a bar.

00:07:53.272 --> 00:07:55.340
-It's true!
I was relieved.

00:07:55.340 --> 00:07:57.443
I was relieved, yeah.
It did occur to me

00:07:57.443 --> 00:07:59.044
at a certain point --
I mean, not that I'm, like,

00:07:59.044 --> 00:08:01.814
dragging her around
to bars, you know...

00:08:01.814 --> 00:08:04.750
I wasn't like,
"Oh, I wish I could

00:08:04.750 --> 00:08:07.053
take her for a vodka tonic."
-Yeah, that's --

00:08:07.053 --> 00:08:08.787
You never want to be
in a situation where everybody's

00:08:08.787 --> 00:08:10.556
talking about how hard the
pandemic was, and you're like,

00:08:10.556 --> 00:08:11.957
"I couldn't bring my baby
to a bar."

00:08:11.957 --> 00:08:13.292
-I know.
[ Laughter ]

00:08:13.292 --> 00:08:15.561
"It was impossible."
Yeah.

00:08:15.561 --> 00:08:18.397
-Hey, congratulations
on "Candy,"

00:08:18.397 --> 00:08:19.565
and thank you so much
for being here.

00:08:19.565 --> 00:08:21.366
It was an absolute delight
to have you.

00:08:21.366 --> 00:08:24.002
You guys, give it up
for Melanie Lynskey.

00:08:24.002 --> 00:08:25.938
"Candy" premieres on Hulu
next Monday,

00:08:25.938 --> 00:08:27.840
kicking off a five-night event

00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:29.708
leading up to the finale
on May 13th.

00:08:29.708 --> 00:08:32.044
Stick around. We'll be right
back with Geoffrey Zakarian.

